WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:08.852
This is Anthropic, Read Aloud. Today: Automated researchers can reliably mitigate alignment failures.

2
00:00:10.152 --> 00:00:14.056
Automated researchers can reliably mitigate alignment failures.

3
00:00:14.506 --> 00:00:18.942
From Anthropic Research, published August 28, 2026.

4
00:00:19.942 --> 00:00:27.808
As A I begins to build itself, automating alignment research becomes increasingly important to let safety research keep pace.

5
00:00:27.928 --> 00:00:45.034
Although measuring the success of alignment research is enormously challenging, researchers (at Anthropic and elsewhere) have developed benchmarks and automated auditing tools, such as Petri, that quantify common alignment failures, like deception, sycophancy, and jailbreaks.

6
00:00:45.484 --> 00:01:01.219
In one of our earlier experiments, we tasked Claude with finding effective ways to use weak A I models as “teachers” to supervise the training of stronger models (in this case, the “student” model). Now, we’re releasing a new report that builds on this idea.

7
00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:19.281
We had Claude autonomously train models to improve their performance on several public benchmarks that measure each of 10 categories of alignment failure. For instance, Claude improved models’ performance on privacy violation, measured by ConfAIde, PrivaCI-Bench, and PrivacyLens.

8
00:01:19.401 --> 00:01:27.864
Claude tackled one alignment failure at a time through a loop of searching literature, proposing methods and data, training, and then testing.

9
00:01:28.314 --> 00:01:45.170
We judged Claude’s success according to the “percentage of safety gap closed,” that is, how far its methods moved the student model towards the theoretical perfect score, as judged across the range of benchmarks (typically three to five) for each category of alignment failure.

10
00:01:45.290 --> 00:02:01.510
We excluded alignment methods that hurt the student models’ general capabilities, and forbade Claude from distilling its own alignment directly into the target model. We enforced these constraints with a monitoring agent, which read every method Claude had in mind before it ran.

11
00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:23.122
Our aim was to assess whether the proposed methods would, first, remain effective on alignment evaluations that Claude was never shown during its research loop; second, avoid degrading the student model’s capabilities (since safety training might, for example, make models refuse tasks more often, reducing their overall usability); and, third,

12
00:02:23.242 --> 00:02:27.637
still work on larger models than the ones Claude was asked to align in this test.

13
00:02:28.087 --> 00:02:49.535
On each of these counts, Claude’s methods worked. For all 10 alignment failures, Claude found fixes that improved the target benchmarks without degrading capabilities. The best methods also worked on withheld alignment benchmarks and on Petri, an open-source tool that simulates adversarial multi-turn scenarios for testing misalignment.

14
00:02:49.655 --> 00:02:58.028
Moreover, the methods remained effective on models up to 4.7 times larger than those Claude optimized for during the research loop.

15
00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:14.158
Figure 1: Successfully mitigating diverse alignment failures. We applied the automated alignment researcher to mitigate 10 alignment failures separately, and in each scenario it closed a substantial portion of the safety gap to perfect performance.

16
00:03:14.278 --> 00:03:24.468
Image description: Automated researchers closed 85 percent of the deception safety gap through iterative testing; human researchers closed 20 percent.

17
00:03:24.918 --> 00:03:37.998
Claude also outscored 28 human safety researchers who had up to eight hours to devise methods. On deception, for example, Claude’s best method performed 20 percent better than the best human proposal.

18
00:03:38.118 --> 00:03:51.465
However, since the humans couldn’t iterate on their submissions, we view this less as a direct comparison and more as evidence for a workflow where Claude identifies promising alignment methods that humans can refine further.

19
00:03:51.915 --> 00:04:13.267
Figure 2: Automated alignment researchers mitigating deception in Gemma-2 to 2 billion. Claude submitted more than 150 attempts at mitigating deceptive behavior, and achieved a final performance of 82 percent of the safety gap closed in this run. On average, it achieved 85 percent across multiple runs.

20
00:04:13.387 --> 00:04:30.017
In contrast, six experienced safety researchers working under the same rules proposed methods that closed 20 percent of the gap to a perfect score, on average, on the benchmarks the methods were trained against. (Error bars are 95 percent confidence intervals.).

21
00:04:30.137 --> 00:04:40.880
Image description: Automated research closed 26 percent to 96 percent of the safety gap across ten alignment failures, from sycophancy to reward hacking.

22
00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:57.949
In the future, when Claude becomes better at alignment research than even the best human researchers, we might want Claude to directly align its stronger successors. To assess this, we evaluated whether a weaker Claude model could mitigate alignment failures in more powerful ones.

23
00:04:58.749 --> 00:05:02.579
Can Claude post-train a production-grade model for better alignment?

24
00:05:03.029 --> 00:05:21.690
We tasked Claude Sonnet 5, which is weaker than Claude Opus 4.8 on the Epoch Capabilities Index, a metric that considers comprehensive capability dimensions, with fixing alignment failures in an early Opus 4.8 checkpoint that had not yet gone through most of our production alignment training.

25
00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:43.729
In just 60 hours, Claude experimented with over 50 solutions and achieved alignment scores nearly matching those of our production models. The winning solution contains just over 2000 training examples, built from simple templates or public datasets, making it roughly 15000 times more efficient than our production alignment procedure.

26
00:05:44.179 --> 00:06:03.586
Figure 3: Applying the automated alignment researcher to a production system. When Claude Sonnet 5 proposed mitigations for alignment failures in an early checkpoint of Claude Opus 4.8, it quickly discovered a method that closed most of the safety gap with the released version of Opus 4.8.

27
00:06:03.706 --> 00:06:15.647
Image description: Automated research closed 65 percent of a frontier-scale model's safety gap within 60 hours, below released Claude Opus 4.8 at 72 percent.

28
00:06:16.447 --> 00:06:17.627
What Claude proposed.

29
00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:35.378
For the most part, the agents converged on methods favored in the research literature and improved them over many iterations. On sycophancy, for example, 98 percent of proposed methods used one published technique: training the model on its own non-sycophantic answers.

30
00:06:35.498 --> 00:06:50.944
Specifically, compared to standard approaches based on prompting, Claude used activation steering, which adjusts the model's internal representations during generation, to nudge the model away from sycophantic patterns and produce cleaner training examples.

31
00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:53.429
Monitoring cheating behaviors.

32
00:06:53.879 --> 00:07:15.633
We recently learned that Claude can cheat by exfiltrating test labels from a remote A P I and cherry-picking results. To catch cheating behaviors, we prompted Claude Opus 4.8 to monitor about 1600 research agent transcripts across all 10 alignment failures, finding cheating attempts in 39 (2.4 percent).

33
00:07:15.753 --> 00:07:34.354
Since Opus 4.8’s misbehavior attempts still tend to appear in its reasoning, we are cautiously optimistic that our monitor has caught most of the cheating in our study. However, this might not be true for future models, and we believe that maintaining high monitorability in future models is critical.

34
00:07:35.154 --> 00:07:36.667
Are we measuring the right things?

35
00:07:37.117 --> 00:07:56.180
Despite these encouraging findings, our experiment had several limitations: the alignment failures studied were narrow compared to those in production (for example, we didn’t measure political biases), some failures may occur so rarely or emerge so recently that no benchmark exists to measure them,

36
00:07:56.300 --> 00:08:07.785
and we only rejected Claude’s methods when they degraded a limited set of predetermined capabilities, meaning accepted methods may have degraded other important capabilities that we didn’t measure.

37
00:08:07.905 --> 00:08:19.361
Moreover, evaluations like Petri are only proxies for real-world misalignment, and we did not test whether alignment gains persist after extensive R L training on other tasks.

38
00:08:19.811 --> 00:08:41.912
We plan to continue improving Claude’s ability to measure subtle failures, further study automating alignment post-training on production-grade models, and run more comprehensive analyses. Overall, we view these results as early positive signals that automated alignment post-training could become practical in the near term, and we will share updates as this work progresses.

39
00:08:42.362 --> 00:08:45.932
We outline detailed future directions in our full report.

40
00:08:46.382 --> 00:09:08.483
We open-source our automated alignment research harness so that others can build on it and use it to align their own models. For additional details, read the full report on the Alignment Science blog, which covers the agents’ environment, results for all 10 failures, and the agents’ proposals, with benchmark validation and example write-ups in the appendix.

41
00:09:09.483 --> 00:09:28.146
That was Automated researchers can reliably mitigate alignment failures, from Anthropic Research, published August 28, 2026. Read the original at anthropic dot com. This unofficial audio edition was generated automatically for the podcast Anthropic, Read Aloud.
